---
// src/pages/website.astro
import ProjectLayout from '../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
---
<ProjectLayout title="This Website" slug="website" date="April 11, 2025" tags={['Web Development', 'JavaScript', 'Astro']}>
  <p>
    I have recently become fascinated with glassmorphic UI design, like the one on VisionOS and rumored to arrive to IOS 19. My previous website was a simple google site that was quick to make and, more importantly, free. It was functional, yet bland.
  </p>
  <p>
    I wanted to create a website that truly represented my personality as a creative and tech enthusiast. Therefore, I chose a new tech stack, that I was previously unfamiliar with, based on the astro framework to push myself to create something stunning.
  </p>
  <p>
    Check out the source code on <a href="https://github.com/VittorioPastore/VittorioPastore.github.io">Github</a>!
  </p>
  <hr />
  <p>
    I used the following pinterest post as inspiration for my UI design. I learned along the way that the best way to get this look is to have bright and high-contrast shapes in the background. Otherwise it will look like your "glass" elements are simply static gradients. Other than that, having a blur effect on each element and adding opacity is the simplest way to achieve this beautiful effect
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="media">
    <figure class="fig">
      <div class="fig-frame pin">
        <iframe src="https://assets.pinterest.com/ext/embed.html?id=953074339895381511" title="Pinterest post" height="500" width="500" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      </div>
    </figure>
  </Fragment>
</ProjectLayout>

<style>
  .pin { display: flex; justify-content: center; padding: var(--card-pad); }
  .pin iframe { border: 0; width: min(100%, 500px); height: 500px; border-radius: 8px; }
</style>
